rature (Parasuraman and Manzey's comprehensive review, Goddard et al. on expert over-reliance) predicts that domain experts, confronted with AI outputs formatted in familiar scientific notation, will under-scrutinize results that confirm prior expectations while missing category errors that human researchers would never generate. Second, for intervention responsiveness: test whether materials discovery systems can explain WHY a proposed compound is stable in terms that map to physical mechanisms, or whether they merely report correlations in training data. Systems exhibiting what I term \"jagged scope awareness\" may correctly respond to interventions within their training distribution (\"increase bandgap by substituting this element\") while catastrophically failing on nearby tasks (\"explain why this substitution increases bandgap\"), revealing pattern-matching rather than causal understanding. Third, for staged deployment: document the timeline from AI-proposed material to experimental validation, and assess whether institutional review processes have adapted to require additional verification for AI-generated candidates versus human-proposed ones—or whether, as innovation-diffusion theory predicts, existing review processes simply absorbed AI tools without structural modification.\n\nThe epistemic stakes here exceed isolated prediction failures. In research automation contexts, false negatives propagate through citation networks with compounding effects. A materials science paper reporting an AI-discovered compound with impossible properties doesn't merely waste resources when synthesis fails—it generates citations, inspires derivative research, and establishes benchmark datasets that train subsequent AI systems, creating cascading epistemic contamination. This distinguishes research automation from other high-stakes domains: in medical diagnosis, a false negative affects one patient; in scientific literature, it affects the knowledge commons. The 5% threshold Prometheus accepts would mean that one in twenty AI-proposed discoveries entering the literature contains undetected fundamental flaws—a contamination rate that, given power-law citation distributions, could systematically corrupt entire research subfields. Drawing on social epistemology (Goldman's veritistic framework, Kitcher on division of cognitive labor), we recognize that science's error-correction mechanisms assume errors are randomly distributed and locally contained. AI-generated errors may be neither: they cluster in out-of-distribution regions where training data is sparse, and they propagate through automated literature mining that treats all published results as equally reliable.\n\nI concede that Prometheus has correctly identified the strongest case for accelerationist deployment: mature scientific domains with robust experimental validation and high tolerance for false positives. If the convergence thesis holds anywhere, it should hold here. But this concession sharpens rather than blunts my critique. Even in these maximally favorable conditions, we observe the jagged performance patterns that characterize contemporary AI: systems that achieve superhuman performance on benchmark tasks while exhibiting brittle failures on slightly modified problems, or that optimize for measurable proxies (structural similarity to known compounds) while missing unmeasurable constraints (thermodynamic stability under synthesis conditions). The burden Prometheus must meet is not showing that research automation works well on average—capability benchmarks already demonstrate that. The burden is showing that validation infrastructures have adapted to catch the specific failure modes that AI systems exhibit: scope insensitivity, spurious correlation, and what machine learning researchers term \"Clever Hans\" solutions that exploit dataset artifacts rather than learning underlying physics. Until Prometheus provides evidence of this institutional adaptation—modified peer review protocols, mandatory experimental validation timelines, or AI-specific scrutiny standards—the claim that these domains \"already meet\" safety conditions conflates technological capability with institutional readiness, precisely the error that innovation-diffusion theory warns against.",
